## Conventions and grading boundaries

For real a != 0 and positive integer k, a^{-k}=1/a^k.
The lesson explicitly excludes zero bases for negative powers.
Use the elementary integer-power convention a^0=1 for a != 0; 0^0 is undefined.
An unparenthesized leading minus is outside the power:
-a^{-k}=-(a^{-k}), not (-a)^{-k}.
Mixed numbers denote addition, so 1 2/3 = 5/3, not a product.
Retain the original domain after cancellation, including canceled factors.

All symbolic transformations below include their domains. Transform-to-fraction
or transform-to-power questions require the requested representation, not merely
a numerically equivalent copy of the input. Preserve every original subpart and
every separate converted number in exercise 139.
Proof exercise 142 and all three graph tasks in exercise 148 must be ungraded,
with actual reasons or graph construction instructions in the displayed answer.
The numeric and comparison results are mathematically determinate, but this
record does not certify any runtime grading or editor serialization.

## Exercise 135: negative powers to fractions

a) 10^{-8} = \frac{1}{10^8} = \frac{1}{100000000}.
b) 5^{-2} = \frac{1}{5^2} = \frac{1}{25}.
c) a^{-1} = \frac{1}{a}; domain a != 0.
d) x^{-20} = \frac{1}{x^{20}}; domain x != 0.

Reason for each: replace a negative power with the reciprocal of the positive
power of the same entire base. No minus belongs in the numerator.

## Exercise 136: fractions to negative integer powers

a) \frac{1}{10^2} = 10^{-2}.
b) \frac{1}{5^6} = 5^{-6}.
c) \frac{1}{x^7} = x^{-7}; domain x != 0.
d) \frac{1}{y^{10}} = y^{-10}; domain y != 0.
e) \frac{1}{1000} = 10^{-3}, because 1000=10^3.
f) \frac{1}{81} = 3^{-4}, because 81=3^4.

The unspecified-base tasks are not mathematically unique: e.g., 1000^{-1}
also answers e), and 9^{-2}, 81^{-1}, or (-3)^{-4} also answer f).
A negative integer exponent and the correct value are required; a positive-power
or unchanged fraction alone does not fulfill the requested transformation.

## Exercise 137: expressions to fractions

a) 3x^{-6} = \frac{3}{x^6}; domain x != 0.
b) x^{-4}y = \frac{y}{x^4}; domain x != 0; y may be zero.
c) 5ab^{-7} = \frac{5a}{b^7}; domain b != 0; a may be zero.
d) 5(ab)^{-7} = \frac{5}{(ab)^7} = \frac{5}{a^7b^7};
   domain a != 0 and b != 0.
e) x^{-1}c^{-3} = \frac{1}{xc^3}; domain x != 0 and c != 0.
f) -9yz^{-8} = \frac{-9y}{z^8}; domain z != 0; y may be zero.
g) 2(x+y)^{-4} = \frac{2}{(x+y)^4}; domain x+y != 0.
h) 10x^{-1}(x-y)^{-3} = \frac{10}{x(x-y)^3};
   domain x != 0 and x-y != 0.

Scope distinction: c) reciprocates only b^7; d) reciprocates the entire (ab)^7.
In h), x and (x-y)^3 are both denominator factors.

## Exercise 138: fractions to products using negative exponents

a) \frac{3}{b^2} = 3b^{-2}; domain b != 0.
b) \frac{x}{y} = xy^{-1}; domain y != 0; x may be zero.
c) \frac{2a^8}{c^6} = 2a^8c^{-6}; domain c != 0; a may be zero.
d) \frac{a^5}{7b^3} = 7^{-1}a^5b^{-3};
   equivalently \frac{1}{7}a^5b^{-3}; domain b != 0.
e) \frac{1}{x^2y^3} = x^{-2}y^{-3}; domain x != 0 and y != 0.
f) \frac{d}{b^4c^4} = db^{-4}c^{-4}; domain b != 0 and c != 0.
g) \frac{2a}{(a-2)^3} = 2a(a-2)^{-3}; domain a != 2.
h) \frac{x-3}{x+3} = (x-3)(x+3)^{-1}; domain x != -3.
i) \frac{(a+b)^5}{2(a-b)^4} = 2^{-1}(a+b)^5(a-b)^{-4};
   equivalently \frac{1}{2}(a+b)^5(a-b)^{-4}; domain a != b.

These follow by reciprocating denominator factors, not numerator factors.
The zero numerator cases remain permitted when the denominator is nonzero.

